  "notes": "やる is one of the most common verbs in casual Japanese, with two distinct meanings.\n\nTRANSITIVITY:\n- Type: {他動詞|たどうし} (transitive)\n\nSENSE 1 — TO DO (casual する):\n- {仕事|しごと}をやる: to do work\n- {宿題|しゅくだい}をやる: to do homework\n- やってみる: to give it a try\n- やり{直|なお}す: to redo\n- やり{遂|と}げる: to accomplish, to see through\n\nSENSE 2 — TO GIVE (downward):\nUsed for giving to animals, plants, or those of lower status:\n- {犬|いぬ}に{餌|えさ}をやる: to feed the dog\n- {花|はな}に{水|みず}をやる: to water the flowers\n\nGIVING HIERARCHY:\n- やる: give (to animals/plants/inferiors — casual)\n- あげる: give (to equals — standard)\n- {差|さ}し{上|あ}げる: give (to superiors — humble/polite)\n\nREGISTER:\nやる (sense 1) is informal. In formal contexts, use する instead.",
